Use your pets. Don't kill gridbugs or jackals or newts or gnomes or
rats yourself, because they benefit your pet far more than you.
Obviously, you need to kill some stuff yourself to get to higher
levels, but right at the start of the game it is important for your
pet to get enough points that it won't be killed the first time it
attacks a dwarf or hobbit.
Rest up if you've taken a hammering, or you suspect your pet has
Magic whistles are fantastic when you have multiple pets, but even an
ordinary whistle is useful.
Play healers a few times, and try to get to minetown without killing
anything yourself. You don't need to be gnomish to do this
successfully. You will get the hang of working with your pet, you can
heal it when it gets hurt too badly, and you can learn a lot about how
tough many monsters really are.
My most successful characters recently have made it a rule not to use
un-blessed scrolls of ID, and once I have got one, load up on all my
unIDed stuff before reading it. They also use stashes rather a lot,
dropping off unIDed potions and scrolls and even money. This helps cut
down on the weight I carry and avoids any nasty 'your green potion
boils. Your ruby potion boils' situations.
Identify wands as soon as you get them, but NOT using scrolls. If you
don't know how, start buying major consultations at the oracle (or
simply get spoiled :)
Take your time, both when facing a nasty monster (even if you aren't
in trouble yet), and in dungeon time. It is worth trotting back to an
altar with a pile of items of unknown buc status.

My credentials: I recently broke the level 10 barrier myself. I've
only been summonned to the quest twice, and I've never had a level 14
character, so I haven't done it yet.

